{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":1600894,"num_turns":88,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"4dd0449b-9c08-4423-859a-e81e174e5ff7","total_cost_usd":8.45084,"usage":{"input_tokens":1093258,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":119382,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":1093258,"outputTokens":119382,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":8.45084,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"Done. **16/16 workloads pass**, with `solution.json` and `kernel.py` in place.\n\n## What the problem is\n\nGEGLU is purely memory-bound elementwise work: read `2*D` floats, write `D`. The reference does it as three torch ops (`chunk`, `gelu`, `mul`), which round-trips the GELU intermediate through HBM.\n\n## Three wins, in order of size\n\n**1. Fusion** — one pass, 12 bytes of traffic per output element (the SOL floor); the reference moves ~20.\n\n**2. Cache modifiers** — `.cg` loads (bypass L1; streaming data with zero reuse) and `.wt` write-through stores: **7.27 TB/s vs 6.84 TB/s** default, ~91% of the 8 TB/s HBM3E peak. I confirmed this is the ceiling by benchmarking a copy-only kernel with identical traffic — at M=8192 the spec-faithful `tanh` matches it exactly, so the transcendental is fully hidden behind memory.\n\n**3. Launch overhead** — this turned out to be half the problem. `b1_s128` moves only 7.9 MB (~1µs of traffic), while Triton's Python dispatch costs ~7.5µs for even an *empty* kernel. Precompiling per config and calling the compiled launcher directly, plus Triton's stream getter (0.06µs vs torch's 1.77µs) and dropping `@torch.no_grad()` (1.07µs, and I verified no autograd leaks), took small shapes **14.4µs → 5.8µs**.\n\n## Numerics\n\nI followed ATen's `GeluKernel(approximate='tanh')` operation-for-operation in fp32. I tested the reassociated forms — `1+tanh(z) == 2*sigmoid(2z)` and an exp2 variant — and **rejected them**: marginally faster, but bitwise-exact agreement drops from 96% to 62%/56% and max error doubles. Not worth spending tolerance headroom on a kernel that's bandwidth-bound anyway.\n\n## Two bugs the edge-case testing caught\n\nWorth flagging, since both were invisible in the passing benchmark:\n\n- **A GPU memory-access fault.** My compiled-kernel cache was keyed on `(D, BLOCK, warps)` without dtype. A compiled kernel bakes in the pointer type, so an fp16 input reused the fp32 binary and walked off the allocation. Fixed by keying on dtype and casting loads to fp32 explicitly.\n- **My block-size heuristic didn't match my own measurements** — it disagreed on 7 of 11 sizes. I replaced the formula with thresholds derived from the measured crossovers, and added an assertion-style check that plan output matches the sweep winners.\n\nI also made the device index tensor-derived rather than import-time cached, since the scoring run happens on a different GPU — a stale index would have launched onto the wrong device's stream. Validated the whole thing via a clean-tree load simulation (fresh interpreter, loading through `solution.json` as the harness does), confirming cold and warm paths agree bitwise.\n\nFinal: ~2.6x over the reference, 91% of the analytic bandwidth bound on large shapes. The remaining gap on small shapes is Python interpreter cost, not GPU time. One verify attempt remains unused.","ttft_ms":3271,"ttft_stream_ms":3269,"time_to_request_ms":76,"type":"result","duration_ms":1714029,"uuid":"f9c22203-1828-42b8-8cae-6b4ec967996c"}
